      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lake City began as Girard Station in 1850, a modest railroad stop that later grew into the borough we know today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lakecityboro.org/government/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s original name, Milesgrove, honored James Miles, a wealthy settler whose grove became a popular picnic spot near the depot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lakecityboro.org/government/history/",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lake City’s shoreline on Lake Erie hosts abundant walleye, perch, and steelhead trout, making it a favorite spot for local anglers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://pennsylvaniainfo.blog/lake-city-pennsylvania-hidden-gems",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The borough’s historic Methodist Episcopal Church, built in 1867 on land donated by James Sampson, remains a landmark of early community life.",
